  "title": "だから — so, that’s why",
  "pattern": "A。だから、B。",
  "explanation": "だから opens a new sentence with the result of the one before — a から that stands on its own. In polite speech it’s ですから, and many people use なので the same way at the start of a sentence.",
  "natural": "Drawing a conclusion in conversation: {明日|あした}は{休|やす}み。だから、{出|で}かけよう！ — Tomorrow’s a day off. So let’s go out!",
  "stiff": "Said sharply on its own, だから！ means \"like I said!\" and sounds irritated. In polite talk ですから can come across as lecturing; なので is softer.",
